# Onboarding a new source to an existing tracking plan

_Use this guide when you've already defined a tracking plan in Avo and you're now adding a new platform, brand, or market source to it. If you're starting from a blank tracking plan, the [Start data design](https://www.avo.app/docs/data-design/start-data-design) guide is the right entry point instead._

## When to use this guide

Reach for this guide when:

- You have an existing tracking plan in Avo with events, properties, and (typically) a global taxonomy.
- You're adding a new source — a new platform (e.g. iOS), a new brand, or a new market — that needs to send some subset of those events.
- The source has its own implementation timeline that may not match the rest of the plan, and you may want to break the work into smaller branches.

This is a common pattern when teams are reimplementing tracking, expanding to a new brand, market, or product line, or rolling out Avo in stages across a multi-platform product. If you're starting fresh, use [Start data design](https://www.avo.app/docs/data-design/start-data-design) instead.

## How this fits with the broader Avo workflow

This guide focuses on the Plan and Implement steps of the [Avo workflow](https://www.avo.app/docs/workflow) <!-- REVIEW: confirm canonical URL for the workflow overview doc --> for the specific case of adding a new source. The shape is the same as any other planning work — branch, edit, review, implement, validate, publish — but with source-level decisions (event assignment, property presence per source, branch slicing) layered on top.

## Step 1 — Create the source in Avo

Open a branch and add the new source. See [Define sources and destinations](https://www.avo.app/docs/data-design/avo-tracking-plan/define-sources-and-destinations) for the click-by-click. As you create it, set:

- **Source name and platform** — match how engineering refers to the codebase (e.g. `web-app`, `ios-app`).
- **Destinations** — which destinations this source sends to. Often the same as other sources for the same product.
- **Codegen language and output (if using Codegen)** — see [Avo Codegen overview](https://www.avo.app/docs/implementation/avo-codegen-overview). <!-- REVIEW: confirm canonical URL for Codegen overview --> Configure now so engineering can pick it up later in step 4.

## Step 2 — Assign events from the central taxonomy to the source

On the same branch, walk the event list and add the new source to each event. For larger plans, use bulk-edit on the events list — filter the events you want, select them, and add the source in one operation.

**Branch strategy:** use one branch per logical grouping — often per market, brand, or product line — depending on how your organization slices ownership. If multiple sources within a group have meaningfully different timelines or reviewers, one branch per source is fine, but per-group keeps coordination simpler.

**Configure source-specific property behavior.** Once an event is assigned to the source, you can configure each property on that event differently per source:

- **Presence** — required, optional, or not sent. A property can be required on one source and optional on another.
- **Allowed values (where it applies)** — a constrained list of values for this source. Useful when the same property has source-specific values.

Reference: [Configuring when properties are required or optional](https://www.avo.app/docs/data-design/avo-tracking-plan/properties#configuring-when-properties-are-required-or-optional).

Tips:

- Default to the global presence and only override where the source genuinely behaves differently — fewer overrides means easier maintenance.
- Document the reason for each override in the event description so a future reviewer doesn't have to reverse-engineer it.
- If you find yourself overriding the same property the same way across many events on this source, consider whether the global definition should change instead.

