## Step 3 — Share with data consumers and implementers to review and align on implementation

This is a working session, not just a sign-off. With the analytics owner who'll consume the data and the engineering DRI who'll implement it:

- **Sanity-check the source-specific property behavior** — does it match what the source can actually send?
- **Decide which sources will implement what events, when.** Use this conversation to lock the implementation slices.
- **Slice the work if it won't land at once.** If a source's implementation will roll out in stages, push a subset of changes from this branch into smaller implementation branches using [push and pull between branches](https://www.avo.app/docs/data-design/branches/pulling-pushing-items-between-branches#pushing-items-to-another-branch). Each implementation branch can then move on its own timeline.

Required reviewers can be configured per source so the right people are pulled in automatically — see [Required reviewers](https://www.avo.app/docs/data-design/required-reviewers). <!-- REVIEW: confirm canonical URL for required reviewers -->

## Step 4 — Implement and validate

Once the branch (or implementation slice) is aligned and approved:

- Engineering pulls the latest [Avo Codegen](https://www.avo.app/docs/implementation/avo-codegen-overview) for the source and uses the generated tracking functions.
- Validate in dev and staging using the inline debugger <!-- REVIEW: add link to inline debugger doc once URL is confirmed --> and the [Avo Inspector debugger](https://www.avo.app/docs/inspector/avo-inspector-overview).
- Once green in dev/staging, the branch is ready to merge.

**Dependency:** Codegen and Inspector must be set up on the source first. If they aren't yet, prioritize that before starting implementation.

## Step 5 — Publish

Publish each Avo branch to main once the corresponding git branch is merged. If you're using direct publishing destinations (e.g. a webhook, cloud storage such as S3 or GCS, or a destination connector), confirm the publish completes after the merge.

If you broke the work into multiple implementation branches, repeat steps 3–5 for each.

## Common pitfalls

- **Assigning a source to every event by default.** Saves time today, costs accuracy forever. Be deliberate.
- **Treating source-specific overrides as the rule, not the exception.** If you have many overrides on one source, the global definition is probably wrong.
- **Merging to main before implementation lands.** Inspector will report drift between plan and production until the code catches up. Keep the branch open until the slice is implemented.
- **Skipping push/pull and copying work manually between branches.** Manual copies drift. Use push to keep the source of truth on one branch.

